## 1. Introduction
With the advancement of society and the rapid development of radio communication technology, People have entered the era of the fifth-generation (5G) mobile communication technology. A wide variety of electronic products bring convenience to modern life. However, serious EM pollution is endangering human health and living environment [1–3]. In addition, with the globalization of today’s world, all countries are striving to enhance their national defense. Stealth technology can be used as a new weapon attack and protection measures [4]. Therefore, it is necessary to explore high performance MA materials. Excellent absorbing materials should have the advantages of ‘‘thin thickness, light weight, strong adsorption, and wide bandwidth” [5,6]. However, it is difficult for traditional materials to satisfy all these requirements due to their limited attenuation capacity and impedance mismatch [7]. Therefore, it is a new breakthrough to design new structural and multi-component materials with excellent absorbing properties. Scheme 1.
It is widely known that carbon-based materials are widely used in MA domains due to their high conductivity, stable properties and low preparation cost. Unfortunately, the impedance mismatch caused by the single electrical loss mechanism and their narrow absorption bandwidth have affected their performance [8,9]. However, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), which are composed of metal clusters and organic ligands, have proven to be remarkable candidate materials for high-performance microwave absorbers, owing to their high porosity, large specific surface area, adjustable microstructure and paramagnetism [10–12]. Carbon-based magnetic composites can be produced by in-situ pyrolysis of MOFs, retaining their electrical conductivity and stability [13–15]. Moreover, many studies have reported that the micro-nanostructures derived from MOFs are very effective for microwave absorption. For example, the core–shell, hollow and three- dimensional layered structures have become research hotspots in recent years [16,17]. On the one hand, the interface vacancies of these structures regulate their high complex permittivity, and serve as polarization centers. On the other hand, their porous or hollow microstructures can induce more free spaces and interfaces to promote multiple reflection behaviors of incident EM waves, which can effectively reduce the permittivity and enhance loss ability to ameliorate the impedance matching [18,19]. For example, Qiu and co-workers synthesized a hollow nickel/carbon (Ni/C) microsphere composite through pyrolysis of Ni-MOF in an argon atmosphere [20]. Liu et al. prepared porous and hexagonal sheet-like $\mathsf{C o@C}$ composites. When the filling rate was $70\mathrm{wt\%}$ , the maximum $\mathtt{R_{L}}$ of the core–shell composites reached 53.8 dB, and the matching EAB was $5.4\mathsf{G H z}$ [21]. In order to optimize this high filler content, the derivatives with hetero-bimetallic MOFs as precursors have also been considered as microwave absorbers. Wang et al. prepared hierarchical CoFe $@{\cal C}$ composite structure by high temperature pyrolysis under $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ atmosphere. The absorber displayed an optimal $\mathtt{R_{L}}$ of $-61.8$ dB and a corresponding EAB of up to $9.2\mathsf{G H z}$ at $10\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ filler loading [22]. Yang et al. obtained bimetallic ${\sf C o}/$ Zn-MOF by doping metal ions and prepared 3D hierarchical rambutan-like bimetallic/carbon@carbon nanotubes composites by pyrolysis in argon [23]. The experimental results indicated that the introduction of other metal ions moderately adjusted the porosity and impedance matching, resulting in a lower packing load and high EM wave absorption strength [24]. As a typical representative of nano-ions in magnetic alloys, NiCo alloys display unique magnetic anisotropy, high saturation magnetization and excellent permeability, which is crucial to obtain highperformance absorbers [25]. In conclusion, the magnetic metal carbon composites derived from MOFs have a synergistic loss mechanism of magnetic loss and dielectric loss. Carbon matrix composites with different structures also provide more possibilities for the preparation of light absorbers.
In this paper, a NiCo-MOF synthesized by solvothermal method was used as a template and carbonized at $600^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in an argon atmosphere to obtain a ${\mathsf{N i C o@C}}$ composite with a plum-like structure. The formation of a graphitized carbon layer was further promoted by NiCo-nano ions as catalysts. More importantly, the pyrolysis of MOFs can control the uniform distribution of magnetic metals and carbon materials. The carbon layer is coated with internal magnetic ions, preventing the oxidation of magnetic metal ions and offering a channel for electron transport [26,27]. The MA properties of different loadings of ${\mathsf{N i C o@C}}$ in the range of $2{\cdot}18~\mathrm{GHz}$ were studied systematically. Encouragingly, the polyhedral ${\mathsf{N i C o@C}}$ exhibits excellent MA performance in wide absorption bandwidth $(7.2\mathsf{G H z})$ and strong absorption capacity (-55.4 dB). The combination of the electrical loss of carbon and the magnetic loss of NiCo alloy improves the impedance matching performance of ${\mathsf{N i C o@C}}$ effectively widening the absorbing band of the composite. The results also show that the plum-like structure ${\mathsf{N i C o@C}}$ is an excellent MA absorber because of its shape anisotropy, porous structure, thin thickness, and strong absorption capacity. At the same time, this work also opens up a new way for the artificial synthesis of alloy/carbon composites with porous nanostructures.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Materials and chemicals
Cobalt nitrate hexahydrate $(\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}.6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ , AR, $99\%$ in purity, aladdin), nickel nitrate hexahydrate $(\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ , AR, $99\%$ in purity, aladdin), 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylic acid $\mathrm{(C_{9}H_{6}O_{6}}$ , $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C}$ $98\%$ in purity, aladdin), polyvinylpyrrolidone $\mathsf{I}({\mathsf{C}}_{6}\mathsf{H}_{9}\mathsf{N O})_{\mathrm{n}},$ PVPK30, macklin]. ethanol $(99.8\%$ in purity, tianjin) and N, $N\mathrm{.}$ dimethylformamide $\big(\mathrm{HCON}(\mathrm{CH}_{3})_{2}$ , DMF, $99.5\%$ in purity, Sinopharm) were analytical grade. All chemicals and reagents are provided by Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. and received for use no further refinement.
## 2.2. Preparation of plum-like NiCo@C composite
## 3. Results and discussion
The crystal structures and phase composition of the ${\mathsf{N i C o@C}}$ composite were investigated by XRD analysis, and the results are observed in Fig. 1a. The XRD pattern reveals that the sample has three typical diffraction peaks centered at $44.6^{\circ}$ , $51.0^{\circ}$ and $74.6^{\circ}$ . These are located between the cubic crystal structures of Ni and Co, which is coincide with the (110), (200), and (220) planes of the NiCo alloy, stating clearly the formation of NiCo nanoparticles [28–30]. The sample does not show the diffraction peak of crystalline carbon in the XRD spectrum after high temperature treatment, which may be attributed to the low crystallinity of carbon. Raman spectroscopy is an effective analytical method for detecting the presence and bonding state of carbon in the materials [31]. There are two distinguishable characteristic Raman peaks of carbon material in the ${\mathsf{N i C o@C}}$ composite (Fig. 1b), which are located at approximately 1353 and $1584~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , and the peak position matches well with the D-band and G-band of carbon materials. The D-band indicates the disordering of the carbon structure and atomic crystal defects, and the G-band is the main characteristic peak of carbon materials caused by the stretching vibration of $\mathsf{\bar{s p}}^{2}$ hybridized carbon atoms [32]. Generally, the ratio of the Dband and G-band $\left(\mathrm{I_{D}}/\mathrm{I_{G}}\right)$ is generally used to represent the graphitization degree of carbon components, with smaller $\mathrm{I_{D}/I_{G}}$ ratios representing a higher degree of graphitization. However, the $\mathrm{I_{D}/I_{G}}$ value of the sample is 0.92, indicating that the ${\mathsf{N i C o@C}}$ composite has a low degree of graphitization and a large carbon defect concentration, which can increase the polarization loss.
To further analyze the chemical composition and elemental status of ${\mathsf{N i C o@C}}$ composites (Fig. 2), XPS was employed as a diagnostic tool. As shown in Fig. 2a, the full survey spectrum visually demonstrates the existence of C, Co and Ni elements. The Fig. 2b shows the high- resolution spectral peaks of the C 1 s, where the main three carbon-containing peaks at 284.8 eV, 285.8 eV, and $287.6\ \mathrm{eV}$ are attributed to the ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathsf{C}}/{\mathsf{C}}{\mathrm{=}}{\mathsf{C}}$ , CAO and $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ . The fitted Co 2p spectrum (Fig. 2c) can be decomposed into four obvious Co peaks. The peaks at 778.5 and $780.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ correspond to Co $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ , while the other two principal peaks at 791.4 and 797.4 eV are assigned to Co $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ [33–35]. As seen in Fig. 2d, the highresolution spectra of $\mathsf{N i}2\mathsf{p}$ can be decomposed into two peaks of $853.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ for $\mathsf{N i}^{0}\ 2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and $870.3{\ensuremath{\mathrm{~eV}}}$ for $\mathsf{N i}^{0}\ 2\mathsf{p}_{1/2}$ , and other two peaks of 855.7 and $874.4\ \mathrm{eV}$ are ascribed to $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}\ 2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ , respectively. The satellite peaks of $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ species are located at 861.1 and $879.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ . The Co 2p and Ni 2p spectra clearly indicate that the surface metal atoms of the NiCo alloy exist in an oxidized state [36].
SEM and TEM images were used to observe the morphology and microstructure of the ${\mathsf{N i C o@C}}$ composite, as shown in Fig. 3. Fig. 3a shows the NiCo-MOF particles have a smooth surface with an average diameter of $700-800\mathrm{nm}$ . The formation of the smooth precursor is due to the fact that PVP is instrumental in forming regular spherical materials and avoiding product aggregation as a stabilizer and dispersant [37]. In this paper, nickel nitrate and cobalt nitrate are used as the bimetallic resources to synthesize NiCoMOF. As an organic linker, $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C}$ is more favorable for the coordination of organic ligands and metal ions in DMF solvent. In addition, PVP encapsulates the nickel ions, cobalt ions and $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C}$ to form spherical NiCo-MOF during the solvothermal reaction [20]. Fig. 3b-d shows that the morphology of the ${\mathsf{N i C o@C}}$ composite derived from NiCo-MOF significantly changes with a rough surface and porous structure after the annealing treatment. During the annealing process, the reduction of metal ions by carbon accelerates the decomposition of organic ligands, which is the main reason for the structural change. As a result, obvious voids appear in the MOF, making the structure easy to condense. The size of the ${\mathsf{N i C o@C}}$ composite reduces to about $500~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , possibly due to the waste of rich organic components and the partial assembly of $\lvert\mathrm{Ni}\rvert$ Co crystals [38]. In addition, the decomposition of organic ligands produces gas during annealing, leading to abundant pores in materials. In general, the reduction of volume and the obtained plumlike structure increase the specific surface area, which is conducive to multiple reflecting and scattering of incident EM waves.
TEM images were used to investigated the details of the interior microstructure of the ${\mathsf{N i C o@C}}$ composite (Fig. 3e-h). From Fig. 3(e, f), we can see that the morphology and size of the product basically conform with the SEM pictures. The TEM image (Fig. 3g) at large magnification confirms that the composite is composed of many small particles. The visible lattice spacing of $0.203\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 3h) can be well indexed to the (111) plane of NiCo alloy, demonstrating the formation of NiCo alloy particles during the pyrolysis process under the protecting of an argon atmosphere [39]. A carbon layer at the edge of the NiCo alloy is also visible, tightly wrapping around the particles. Energy dispersive spectroscopy (EDS) elemental mapping is revealed in Fig. 3j-l, proving the uniform distribution of C, Co and Ni. The Co, Ni and C almost completely overlap, indicating that the NiCo alloy is uniformly dispersed and surrounded by a highly conductive carbon layer. Consequently, there are abundant interfaces between the NiCo crystals and the carbon matrix, which may potentially lead to the enhanced interfacial polarization and stronger EM attenuation.
With the aim of illustrating the porosities of as-obtained materials, BET measurement is utilized to analyze. The nitrogen adsorption–desorption curves and the relevant pore distribution are provided in Fig. S1. The BET specific surface area of ${\mathsf{N i C o@C}}$ (Fig. S1a) is $96.07~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ and the total pore volume is $0.03~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ The adsorption–desorption curve demonstrates a typical tape-IV isotherm, indicating that the sample has a mesoporous structure [40]. From the Fig. S1b, it can be observed that the pore size of the sample is mainly distributed in the range of $2-3~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . The porous structure both reduces the density of the absorbing material and provides more active centers for polarization and scattering, thereby improving the absorbing performance of the material [41,42].
As we all know, different filler loads have a great influence on the absorbing performance of materials. To evaluate the electromagnetic absorption characteristics of ${\mathsf{N i C o@C}}$ , the $\mathsf{R}_{\mathrm{L}}$ value of different filler loads ( $10\mathrm{\Omega}\mathrm{wt}\%$ , $20\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ and $30\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ ) was calculated using transmission line theory. It is generally acknowledged that $90\%$ of the incident EM waves can be assimilated by a material when its $\mathtt{R_{L}}$ value is less than $-10~\mathrm{dB}$ , and the corresponding frequency range is the EAB [43]. Fig. 4 presents the $\mathsf{R}_{\mathrm{L}}$ curves and the three-dimensional $\mathsf{R}_{\mathrm{L}}$ map of ${\mathsf{N i C o@C-10}}$ , ${\mathsf{N i C o@C-20}}$ , and $\mathsf{N i C o@C-30}$ with thicknesses of $1.5{-}4.5~\mathrm{mm}$ at $2.0{-}18.0~\mathrm{GHz}$ The optimum filler loading of the composites is $20\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ , with the $\mathsf{N i C o@C-20}$ composite showing the maximum $\mathsf{R}_{\mathrm{L}}$ value of 55.4 dB at 15.3 GHz and the widest EAB of $7.2~\mathrm{GHz}$ (11.8– $18{\:}{\mathrm{GHz}}{\mathrm{,}}$ ) with a thickness of $2.5~\mathrm{mm}$ (Fig. 4c-d). When the filler loading reduces to $10\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ (Fig. 4a-b), the optimal $\mathtt{R_{L}}$ value of the composite is $-20.7$ dB with an EAB of $8.7\mathsf{G H z}$ at a thick thickness of $4.0\ \mathrm{mm}$ . Besides, from Fig. 4e and f, the maximum $\mathtt{R_{L}}$ value and EAB of ${\mathsf{N i C o@C-30}}$ $(30~\mathrm{wt\%})$ increases to $-29.5$ dB and $7.3~\mathrm{GHz}$ $(11.7\mathrm{-}18~\mathrm{GHz})\$ , respectively. On the basis of the above datas, it is worth emphasizing that the microwave absorption strength will not increase monotonically with the increase of packing load, and the best $\mathsf{R}_{\mathrm{L}}$ value may not be obtained at a higher load rate, which provides the possibility of reducing the weight of the absorber. Fig. 4 (g, h) reveals the maximum $\mathsf{R}_{\mathrm{L}}$ value and EAB of all samples with the different thicknesses to better understand their microwave absorption performance. All the samples have the similar EAB, while $\mathsf{N i C o@C-10}$ and $\mathsf{N i C o@C-30}$ have lower microwave absorption intensities and larger thicknesses than ${\tt N i C o@C-20}$ In conclusion, ${\tt N i C o@C-20}$ has the advantages of strong EM wave absorption capacity, a thin absorbing coating thickness and a wide EAB $7.2\mathsf{G H z}$ , covers the whole Ku-band), which can be considered as an excellent alternative to microwave absorbing materials. Furthermore, it should not be overlooked that the thickness of the absorber has a significant influence on microwave absorption. As the thickness raise, the maximum $\mathtt{R_{L}}$ value will gradually shifts to low frequencies. This phenomenon can be explicated by the quarter wavelength elimination model. And the connection between matching thickness $\left(\mathrm{{t}_{\mathrm{{m}}}}\right)$ and frequency $\left(\mathrm{f_{m}}\right)$ can be expressed by the formula [44,45]:
$$
t_{m}={\frac{n c}{4f_{m}\sqrt{\varepsilon_{\gamma}\mu_{\gamma}}}}\ (n=1,\ 3,\ 5\ ...)
$$
The red curve in Fig. 5b is the simulation curve $(\lambda/4)$ of the $\mathsf{N i C o@C-20}$ composite, marked as $\bf{t}_{\mathrm{m}}^{\mathrm{fit}}$ . The black squares in the Fig. 5b correspond to the thickness of each $\mathsf{R}_{\mathrm{L}}$ peak, and each square is exactly on the red curve. It shows that the relationship between $\mathbf{t}_{\mathrm{m}}$ and $\mathbf{f}_{\mathrm{m}}$ basically follows the quarter-wavelength elimination model and means that the $180^{\circ}$ phase difference exists between the incident and reflected wave of the absorber. Therefore, electromagnetic waves can be attenuated by the interference cancellation effect caused by incident and reflected waves [46].
It is well known that EM parameters play a decisive role in microwaves loss. In order to further explore the loss mechanisms of EM waves in the ${\mathsf{N i C o@C}}$ composite, the electromagnetic parameters of the materials with different filler loadings $(10\mathrm{wt\%},20\mathrm{wt\%}$ , $30\mathrm{\ut\%})$ were evaluated. The images of the relative complex permittivity $\varepsilon_{\gamma}(\varepsilon_{\gamma}=\varepsilon^{\prime}-j\varepsilon^{\prime\prime})$ and complex permeability $\mu_{\gamma}$ $(\mu_{\gamma}=\mu^{\prime}-j\mu^{\prime\prime})$ are shown in Fig. 6. Generally speaking, the $\varepsilon^{\prime}$ and $\mu^{\prime}$ represent the storage capabilities of electric and magnetic energy, while the $\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}$ and $\mu^{\prime\prime}$ denote the dissipation capabilities of a material [47]. Obviously, the value of $\mathbf{\delta}\mathbf{\mathcal{E}^{\prime}}$ (Fig. 6a) decreases from 13.1 to 6.2, 8.1 to 4.1 and 5.0 to 2.2 for ${\mathsf{N i C o@C-10}}$ , NiCo@C-20 and ${\mathsf{N i C o@C-30}}$ , respectively. This phenomenon may be due to the frequency dispersion effect caused by the polarization hysteresis of high-frequency electric field, a typical behavior of electrolytes and many carbon materials [48]. The imaginary parts of the curves also show a similar descending trend in Fig. 6b, which fluctuate from 7.8, 4.7 and 3.1 (for NiCo@C-10, ${\tt N i C o@C-20}$ , and ${\mathsf{N i C o@C-30}};$ to 2.8, 2.1 and 0.9 in the range of $2{\mathrm{-}}18{\mathrm{~GHz}}$ , respectively. The imaginary parts of the curves are relatively flat at high frequencies $(14-18~\mathrm{GHz})$ , indicating stable dielectric loss capability. Fig. 6c shows that as the frequency increases, the $\mu^{\prime}$ curves has a slight downward trend along with fluctuations, and the $\mu^{\prime}$ values of ${\mathsf{N i C o@C}}$ (10 wt%, $20~\mathrm{wt\%}$ , and $30\ \mathrm{wt\%})$ fluctuate from 1.13 to 1.07, 1.18 to 1.02 and 1.21 to 1.06, respectively. The minor fluctuations of real part of permeability also mean that the magnetic energy storage and conversion of the absorber are relatively stable. At the same time, the curves of $\mu^{\prime\prime}$ has a similar decreasing trend in Fig. 6d, and the $\mu^{\prime\prime}$ values of the samples show very little difference, illustrating their similar magnetic loss capability.
Additionally, the ratios of the imaginary and real parts of permittivity and permeability are tan $\delta_{\mathrm{{g}}}$ $(\mathtt{t a n}\delta_{\varepsilon}=\mathtt{c}^{\prime\prime}/\mathtt{c}^{\prime}$ ) and tan $\updelta_{\upmu}$ $({\tan\delta}_{\mu}=\mu^{\prime\prime}/\mu^{\prime}$ ), respectively. Usually, tan $\delta_{\mathrm{{g}}}$ is used to evaluate the dielectric loss, and the appearance of multiple resonance peaks in the curves (Fig. 6e) can interpret the polarization mechanism of the absorber. Generally speaking, the dielectric loss mechanism can be divided into conductance loss and polarization loss, and the conductance loss is mainly the dissipation of induced current in the form of heat. Polarization loss includes interface polarization, dipole polarization, ion polarization and electron polarization. However, ion polarization and electron polarization can be ignored because they usually occur at higher frequencies $(10^{3}–10^{\bar{6}}~\mathrm{GHz})$
[49]. Therefore, in this work, interface polarization and dipole polarization are the governing loss mechanisms of the ${\mathsf{N i C o@C}}$ microwave absorber. Interface polarization is caused by the large specific surface area, possible structural defects, molecular rotation, and the uneven distribution charge on heterogeneous or phase interfaces, while the formation of dipole polarization is due to the deflection or shift of the functional groups on the electric field [50]. In addition, magnetic loss also plays an indispensable role in the absorption of EM waves. The value of tan $\updelta_{\upmu}$ is usually used to evaluate the magnetic loss capability. Magnetic loss mainly originates from natural and exchange resonance, eddy current resonance and hysteresis effects. Hysteresis effect is caused by irreversible magnetization at high frequencies, so it can not be considered at frequencies of $2{\mathrm{-}}18\ \mathrm{GHz}$ [51]. Generally, the resonance peak in the low frequency range $(<10~\mathsf{G H z})$ is created by natural resonance, while the exchange resonance is more relevant in the high frequency range $({>}10\ \mathsf{G H z})$ [52]. As demonstrated in Fig. 6ef, the value of tan $\delta_{\mathrm{{g}}}$ is much higher than that of tan $\updelta_{\upmu}$ meaning that the dielectric loss dominates the overall in the EM absorption mechanism of the ${\mathsf{N i C o@C}}$ composite.
We further study the dielectric and magnetic properties of the absorber in order to grasp its internal attenuator mechanism, which can be explained by the Cole-Cole plots and ${\sf C}_{0}$ curves in Fig. 7. The complex permittivity is defined as follows [49]:
$$
\varepsilon_{r}^{\prime}=\varepsilon_{\infty}+\frac{\varepsilon_{s}-\varepsilon_{\infty}}{1+\left(2\pi f\right)^{2}\tau^{2}}
$$
$$
\varepsilon_{r}^{\prime\prime}=\frac{2\pi f\tau(\varepsilon_{s}-\varepsilon_{\infty})}{1+\left(2\pi f\right)^{2}\tau^{2}}+\frac{\sigma}{\varepsilon_{0}\omega}
$$
Where $\varepsilon_{s},\varepsilon_{\infty}$ and $\boldsymbol{\omega}$ signify the relative permittivity at the highfrequency limit, and static permittivity and the angular frequency, respectively, and $\tau$ is the polarization relaxation time. The relationship between $\boldsymbol{\varepsilon}^{\prime}$ and $\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}$ is obtained by combining and simplifying the above-mentioned two formulas [53].
$$
\left(\varepsilon^{\prime}-\frac{\varepsilon_{s}+\varepsilon_{\infty}}{2}\right)^{2}+\left(\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}-\frac{\sigma}{\varepsilon_{0}\omega}\right)^{2}=\left(\frac{\varepsilon_{s}-\varepsilon_{\infty}}{2}\right)^{2}
$$
Thence, the two-dimensional $\varepsilon^{\prime}-\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}$ curves can be used to explicate the process of Debye relaxation. Fig. 7a-c shows that there are multiple semicircles on the curves of each ${\mathsf{N i C o@C}}$ composite (with $10\mathrm{\:wt\%}$ $20~\mathrm{wt\%}$ and $30~\mathrm{wt\%}$ filler ratios). Each semicircle indicates one polarization relaxation process. The $\varepsilon^{\prime}-\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}$ curve of $\mathsf{N i C o@C-20}$ and $\mathsf{N i C o@C-30}$ have more obvious semicircles, indicating that there are multiple relaxation processes, which strengthen the dielectric loss of the absorber. Simultaneously, the rising tails of the curves extend to the upper right corner of the graphs, which can be regarded as conductance loss. The effect of conductance loss becomes stronger as the tails grow. Furthermore, the $\varepsilon^{\prime}-\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}$ curve of $\mathsf{N i C o@C-20}$ has a larger slope, potentially because this material has a more uniform conductive network [16]. The multipolarization relaxation process in the Cole-Cole plot mainly comes from interface polarization and dipole polarization. The NiCo nanoparticles are encapsulated in a carbon matrix, resulting in interfaces such as NiCo/C, amorphous and graphite carbon. The polarization of these heterogeneous interfaces provides abundant phase interface sites, which promotes the accumulation of electric charge and the repetitious reflection and dispersion of EM waves. At the same time, the natural dipole moments of functional groups vibrate under the action of alternating electromagnetic fields, improving the loss ability of dipole polarization [54]. In conclusion, we speculate that the superb microwave absorption function of the ${\mathsf{N i C o@C}}$ is inseparable from its conductivity loss and electrolyte loss. Moreover, eddy current loss is a significant magnetic loss mechanism, and it is usually evaluated by ${\sf C}_{0}$ $C_{0}=\mu^{\prime\prime}(\mu^{\prime})^{-2}f^{-1})$
values. If the value of ${\sf C}_{0}$ is stable and does not change with frequency, the eddy current loss is a major contributor to microwave absorption. On the contrary, natural resonance and exchange resonance are the main magnetic loss mechanisms in the range of $2-$ $18~\mathrm{{GHz}}$ in this work [55,56]. It can be seen from Fig. 7d that the ${\sf C}_{0}$ values drop sharply at $2{\cdot}13\ \mathsf{G H z}$ , suggesting that the main mechanisms of magnetic loss in this frequency range are natural and exchange resonance. The curve change is relatively stable within the range of $13{-}18~\mathrm{GHz}$ , indicating that the eddy current loss is relevant in this frequency range.
The key factors that determine the performance of EM wave absorbers are impedance matching and loss factor. The impedance coefficient and attenuation constant of ${\mathsf{N i C o@C}}$ were calculated, as shown in Fig. S2. First of all, the good matching characteristic of an absorber requires the input impedance to match the free impedance of air, so that incident waves can be absorbed as much as possible instead of being reflected from the absorber surface [57]. The impedance matching of the samples can be determined by the equation:
$$
Z=\left|\frac{Z_{i n}}{Z_{0}}\right|=\sqrt{\left|\frac{\mu_{\gamma}}{\varepsilon_{\gamma}}\right|}\mathrm{tanh}\left[j\left(\frac{2\pi f d}{c}\right)\sqrt{\mu_{\gamma}\varepsilon_{\gamma}}\right]
$$
In view of the transmission line theory, if impedance matching is under ideal conditions, the impedance matching value $\left(\left|Z_{i n}/Z_{0}\right|\right)$ is wirelessly close to or equal to 1.0 [16]. According to previous research, when the Z value is between 0.8 and 1.2, it can be considered that most of EM waves can enter the absorber without air reflection [58]. It is known from Fig. S2a that the impedance coefficient of ${\mathsf{N i C o@C-10}}$ is much higher than 1 at high frequencies due to the non-equalization of electromagnetic parameters. For $\mathsf{N i C o@C-20}$ and ${\mathsf{N i C o@C-30}}$ , when the Z value is about 0.8–1.2, the former has a wider absorption bandwidth, containing almost the entire X and $\mathtt{K u}$ bands, and the latter only covers the most of X bands. Moreover, the total loss capacity can also be evaluated by attenuation constant $(\alpha)$ . The decay constant equation is shown below [59]:
$$
\alpha=\frac{\sqrt{2}\pi f}{c}\sqrt{\left(\mu^{\prime\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}-\mu^{\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime}\right)+\sqrt{\left(\mu^{\prime\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}-\mu^{\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime}\right)^{2}+\left(\mu^{\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}+\mu^{\prime\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime}\right)^{2}}}
$$
According to equation (6), the $\alpha$ values (Fig. S2b) of the samples with filler ratios of $10\%$ to $30\%$ sequentially increase. Because the value of $\boldsymbol{\mathfrak{a}}$ is positively correlated with $\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}$ and $\mu^{\prime\prime}$ , higher $\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}$ and $\mu^{\prime\prime}$ values will result in a higher $\alpha$ value. However, the maximum $\mathtt{R_{L}}$ value of the $20~\mathrm{wt\%}$ sample is significantly higher than that of the $30~\mathrm{wt\%}$ sample. Therefore, it can be concluded that ${\mathsf{N i C o@C-}}$ 20 has better impedance matching and stronger attenuation loss, resulting in excellent MA performance.
In summary, the EM wave attenuation mechanisms of the ${\mathsf{N i C o@C}}$ composite are exhibited in Scheme 2. Firstly, as we know, the performance of absorber is inextricably related to the dielectric loss, magnetic loss, impedance matching and attenuation characteristics of materials [55]. In particular, impedance matching determines whether the incident EM waves enter into the materials or are reflected by the surface back into the air. The plum-like ${\mathsf{N i C o@C}}$ composite has abundant pores, which is beneficial to adjust the dielectric constant, thereby optimizing impedance matching. This inference can be explained by the Maxwell Garnett formula [56,60]:
$$
\varepsilon_{e f f}^{M G}=\varepsilon_{1}\frac{(\varepsilon_{2}+2\varepsilon_{1})+2f(\varepsilon_{2}-\varepsilon_{1})}{(\varepsilon_{2}+2\varepsilon_{1})-f(\varepsilon_{2}-\varepsilon_{1})}
$$
In equation (7), $\varepsilon_{1}$ and $\varepsilon_{2}$ represent the dielectric constants of solid and gas, respectively, and $f$ is the volume fraction of gas in the porous materials. It can be seen that the dielectric constant changes with the $f$ value. ${\mathsf{N i C o@C}}$ has a large surface area and results in a large $f$ value, so the impedance matching can be significantly optimized to achieve strong absorption of electromagnetic waves. At the same time, the large surface area and folded structure lead to multiple reflections and scattering of incident EM waves. Secondly, NiCo nanoparticles are evenly encapsulated in the carbon matrix, forming a rich heterogeneous interface, which not only provides a large number of active sites, but also may cause the accumulation of charges, leading to enhanced interface polarization. Furthermore, the defects in the ${\mathsf{N i C o@C}}$ composite can be used as dipole centers, generating dipole polarization and further converting EM waves into thermal energy for dissipation [61]. It’s worth noting that during the high-temperature carbonization process, the formed NiCo particles not only increase the conductivity, but also can act as a catalyst to graphitize carbon in the organic ligand. The evenly distributed carbon matrix forms a unique conductive network that promotes the transmission of electrons, resulting in strong conductive loss [32]. Finally, the highefficiency microwave attenuation characteristics of the absorber are inseparable from the magnetic properties of the NiCo particles, exchange and natural resonance, and eddy current loss [23].
## 4. Conclusion
In conclusion, a plum-like ${\mathsf{N i C o@C}}$ composite with strong MA absorption properties was synthesized through in-situ pyrolysis of the bimetallic MOF. The ${\mathsf{N i C o@C}}$ composite with a $20\%$ filling load ratio has the maximum $\mathtt{R_{L}}$ of $-55.4$ dB when the matching thickness is $2.5~\mathrm{mm}$ , and the corresponding effective absorption width is $7.2~\mathrm{GHz}$ . In general, the excellent MA characteristics of the sample benefits from the uniform distribution of the metal NiCo nanoparticles and carbon matrix, which enhances the interactions among the interfaces and is conducive to electron transmission, and allows matched electromagnetic parameters to be obtained. The synergy between the magnetic loss and the dielectric loss leads to the optimization of impedance matching and improves the attenuation ability of electromagnetic wave. This work shows that the ${\mathsf{N i C o@C}}$ composite derived from bimetallic MOFs exhibits significant advantages in microwave absorption, and it is expected to provide inspiration for the future development of high-performance MA materials.
